Issue: The harness derives the answer once and answers whatever that one conversation wrote, although a derivation of one case is not stable and the model has never been asked for a second one. Over the lineage's fourteen evaluated runs (2059 samples, 14 x 260) the two samples of one question wrote the same answer in 105 of USPTO's 420 question-runs and 580 of LawBench's 700, and the answers the two agreed on were the right ones 0.57 (USPTO) and 0.58 (LawBench) of the time against 0.09 and 0.19 for the ones they disagreed on -- the same split on Symptom2Disease is 666 of 700 agreeing, 0.90 right, against 0.32 for the 34 that disagreed. The solver makes 2.00 (LawBench) / 2.18 (Symptom2Disease) / 3.35 (USPTO) of the 32 model calls a sample is allowed, so a second derivation costs nothing the runner does not already offer. The lineage's only second look at the answer is N5's re-derivation pass (72d9d01), which re-decided the answer rather than reading it and overrode 14 correct Symptom2Disease drafts, while the passes N6-N9 kept are inert on the current traces: run over the parent's own 260 samples, the conform pass returns the answer's own entries in every sample it is asked about (1 LawBench, 0 Symptom2Disease and 2 USPTO answers changed), so the score is decided by the single conversation the sample runs.

Modification: the answer is derived twice and the harness answers the derivation the memory's own writing supports, without deciding anything about the case. `solver/passes.py`: `memory_support_reader` reads, from the frozen memory alone, how much of an answer the memory writes -- a memory that writes names (it repeats an answer string, or writes its answers out of strings its own list carries) is read by the share of the answer's entries its list writes, a memory whose answers are all different is read by the share of the answer's text its own answers are written from -- and `text_ngrams` is the reading behind the second of those. `solver/agent.py`: `ConformedAgent.derive` runs one conversation, `derivation` runs the same question a second time and keeps the first when the two derivations wrote the same entries (read with the memory's joiner, `same_answer`) or when the first answer is one the memory's list writes out in full (`fully_written`, where a second derivation could only tie and so is not made), and otherwise answers the derivation with the higher support; `build_agent` passes the reader in. No model call is added to a sample whose first answer the memory writes in full (0.83 of the parent's LawBench answers, 0.96 of its Symptom2Disease answers, none of USPTO's, whose memory writes each answer once), no dataset name, label list or keyword is used, and no branch on task identity is taken.

Results: {"evaluation_result": {"USPTO": 0.1833, "Symptom2Disease": 0.87, "LawBench": 0.54, "average": 0.5311, "status": "ok"}, "message": "The mechanism did what it was built to do and the average fell 0.0445 from the parent's 0.5756, which one run cannot attribute to it: the second derivation was made in 48/60 USPTO, 25/100 LawBench and 6/100 Symptom2Disease samples (the gate held in the rest), the harness took the second derivation in 14 USPTO and 7 LawBench samples, and inside the run the choice moved the answers the first derivation got wrong by +2 on LawBench ([[LawBench/conversations/0012/sample-1.json]] picks the listed 生产、销售伪劣产品 over 生产、销售伪劣产品罪, [[LawBench/conversations/0036/sample-1.json]] the listed 拐卖妇女、儿童 over 拐卖妇女) and by 0 on USPTO, where 12 of 60 first derivations are the gold against 11 of the 60 answers answered and the criterion kept the right derivation in only 2 of the 6 disagreements where exactly one was the gold ([[USPTO/conversations/0023/sample-0.json]] and [[USPTO/conversations/0023/sample-1.json]] keep a derivation missing a group or the reagent where the other derivation wrote the gold). The fall is the run's own churn: the first derivations alone, the computation the parent makes, score 12/60, 51/100 and 87/100 against the parent's 16/60, 57/100 and 89/100, and only 12 of the 60 USPTO and 89 of the 100 LawBench answers are written the same as the parent's. The expected effect was therefore not achieved -- the offline reading put the mechanism at +0.0195 on the average, and the run puts it at +0.0038 -- and the cost is real: model calls 220 -> 364 (USPTO), 307 -> 351 (LawBench), 300 -> 313 (Symptom2Disease), the run 639 -> 861 s, and USPTO's mean sample 132.9 -> 216.6 s (max 638.2 -> 740.2 s) against a 900 s sample timeout."}

